<meta type=article>
<title | Calibrating Predicted Probabilities>
<subtitle | A Reproducible Workflow for Post-hoc Calibration>
<author name="Dana Okonkwo" affiliation="Department of Statistics, Western University" orcid="0000-0002-1825-0097" +corresponding />
<author | Priya Raman>
<abstract | Modern classifiers often produce scores that rank well but do not behave as probabilities. We review what calibration means, give a small reproducible workflow for post-hoc calibration on a held-out split, and report the effect of two standard calibration maps on a benchmark task. The procedure is deliberately small enough to inspect and re-run end to end.>
</meta>


<section | Introduction>
A classifier that assigns a probability of 0.8 to an event should be right about
80% of the time when it says so. When that correspondence holds the classifier is
**calibrated**, and its scores can be read as probabilities rather than as bare
rankings. Many high-accuracy models are *not* calibrated: <cite @Guo2017> document
that deep networks grow more confident than they are accurate as they are trained
to convergence.<note | Calibration and accuracy are distinct. A model can rank
every positive above every negative — perfect discrimination — while reporting
probabilities that are systematically too high.>

This note has three aims: to state precisely what calibration is, to give a small
reproducible workflow for *post-hoc* calibration on held-out data, and to show how
two standard calibration maps behave on a benchmark task. Early work on probability
forecasts <cite @Brier1950, @Dawid1982> established the scoring rules we still use.

<section | Background>

<sub-section | What calibration means>
<definition #def:calibration | A classifier is *perfectly calibrated* when, among all instances it assigns predicted probability $p$, the long-run fraction of positives equals $p$, for every $p$ in $[0, 1]$.>

Perfect calibration is an idealisation; in practice we measure the *gap* between
predicted confidence and observed accuracy. Binning the predictions into $M$ equal
intervals $B_1, \dots, B_M$ and comparing per-bin accuracy with per-bin confidence
gives the *expected calibration error*:

<$$ #eqn:ece | \mathrm{ECE} = \sum_{m=1}^{M} \frac{|B_m|}{n} \, \bigl| \, \mathrm{acc}(B_m) - \mathrm{conf}(B_m) \, \bigr| $$>

where $n$ is the number of instances and $|B_m|$ the count in bin $m$. Equation
<ref @eqn:ece> is a weighted average of per-bin gaps; it is zero for a perfectly
calibrated model.

<sub-section | Proper scoring rules>
A *scoring rule* assigns a penalty to a probabilistic forecast once the outcome is
known. It is **proper** when honest reporting minimises the expected penalty. The
Brier score — the mean squared difference between probability and outcome — is the
canonical example.

<theorem #thm:proper name="Propriety of the Brier score">
The Brier score is a strictly proper scoring rule: its expected value, taken over
outcomes $Y \sim \mathrm{Bernoulli}(q)$, is uniquely minimised by the report
$p = q$.
</theorem>

<proof>
Expand the expected score for a single instance: $\mathbb{E}\,[(p - Y)^2] = (p - q)^2 + q(1 - q)$. The second term does not depend on $p$, and the first is a squared distance minimised uniquely at $p = q$. ∎
</proof>

The Brier score decomposes into interpretable parts — a reliability term that
measures miscalibration, a resolution term that rewards sharpness, and an
irreducible uncertainty term:

<align #eqn:brier-decomp>
\mathrm{BS} &= \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (p_i - y_i)^2 \\
&= \text{reliability} - \text{resolution} + \text{uncertainty}
</align>

Post-hoc calibration improves the reliability term in <ref @eqn:brier-decomp>
without touching the model's ranking, so resolution is preserved <cite @NiculescuMizil2005>.

<section | Methods>

<sub-section | The workflow>
The workflow keeps three data splits strictly separate, so that the calibrator is
never fit on data used to evaluate it:

1. fit the classifier on the *training* split;
2. fit a one-dimensional calibration map on the *validation* split, using the classifier's scores as input;
3. report ECE and the Brier score on the untouched *test* split.

<frame title="Assumptions">
We assume the held-out predictions are exchangeable with deployment data, and that
the bin boundaries used to compute ECE are fixed before evaluation — so that bin
membership does not depend on the realised outcome. When these fail, the reported
error understates the true miscalibration.
</frame>

The data flow is summarised in <ref @fig:workflow>.

<mermaid #fig:workflow caption="The calibration workflow. The calibrator is fit only on the validation split, and the test split is touched only for reporting.">
graph LR
  A[Training split] --> B[Fit classifier]
  B --> C[Scores on validation]
  C --> D[Fit calibrator]
  D --> E[Scores on test]
  E --> F[Report ECE and Brier]
</mermaid>

<sub-section | Implementation>
The expected calibration error is a short computation once the predictions are in
hand:

<``` python #code:ece |
import numpy as np

def expected_calibration_error(probs, labels, n_bins=10):
    """Equal-width-bin ECE for binary predictions."""
    edges = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, n_bins + 1)
    bin_of = np.digitize(probs, edges[1:-1])
    ece = 0.0
    for m in range(n_bins):
        mask = bin_of == m
        if not mask.any():
            continue
        confidence = probs[mask].mean()
        accuracy = labels[mask].mean()
        ece += mask.mean() * abs(accuracy - confidence)
    return ece ```>

The function follows <ref @eqn:ece -link> term for term: `mask.mean()` is the bin
weight $|B_m| / n$, and the absolute difference is the per-bin gap.

<section | Results>
On a held-out test split of a credit-default benchmark, both calibration maps
reduce ECE substantially while leaving the Brier score and log-loss nearly
unchanged, consistent with the decomposition above. The figures are collected in
<ref @tab:metrics>.

<csv #tab:metrics caption="Calibration metrics on the test split, before and after post-hoc calibration. Lower is better throughout.">
method,ECE,Brier,log-loss
uncalibrated,0.082,0.141,0.447
Platt scaling,0.031,0.128,0.421
isotonic,0.024,0.126,0.418
</csv>

The reliability diagram in <ref @fig:reliability> tells the same story visually:
the uncalibrated curve bows below the diagonal — over-confidence — and calibration
pulls it back toward it.

<fig #fig:reliability src=reliability-diagram.png | Reliability diagram for the uncalibrated model (grey) and after Platt scaling (blue). Points on the diagonal are perfectly calibrated; points below it are over-confident.>

<blockquote>A low ECE is necessary but not sufficient. Because it averages over bins, ECE can hide compensating errors — over-confidence in one region cancelling under-confidence in another. Always read the reliability diagram alongside the single number.</blockquote>

<section | Discussion>
Post-hoc calibration is cheap, model-agnostic, and easy to get wrong in exactly one
way: letting the calibrator see the evaluation data. The workflow above exists to
make that mistake hard. The whole procedure — three splits, one calibration map, two
metrics — is small enough to re-run end to end, which is the point: a number you
cannot reproduce is not yet evidence.<note | The benchmark, the splits, and the
calibration code are deliberately minimal so the entire pipeline can be inspected in
one sitting. Production use would add cross-validated calibration and uncertainty
estimates on the reported metrics.>

Whether distribution-free guarantees can be attached to the calibrated
probabilities — in the conformal-prediction sense — is a natural next question, and
one the present workflow is small enough to extend.

